window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('c1',{"s":["<section id=\"c1-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · 2016–2026</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Open ad hoc teams: teammate models meet populations</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Ad hoc teamwork</b> · One learner meets unknown teammates with no joint training (Stone et al.); most methods assume a fixed team.</li><li><b>Types and openness</b> · Hidden teammate types (Albrecht et al. 2016) and agent openness (Eck, Doshi, Soh); GPL joins the two.</li><li><b>Learned teammate vectors</b> · LIAM (2021) turns the agent&#x27;s own history into a teammate vector; GPL&#x27;s journal and NAHT reuse it.</li><li><b>Diverse partner pools</b> · FCP and MEP train on many self-play partners (Overcooked); NAHT and HOLA borrow the idea.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Team members join, leave or are strangers. A learner we control must cooperate with whoever is present, with no joint training.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Learn policies for changing teams: graph values (GPL), team embeddings (NAHT), partner pools (HOLA); Open HRC learns the reward from demos.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/c1_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Solid = builds on or reuses. Not drawn: journal, MEP, COLE, Open HRC, HOLA. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">Open team</b> · agents may join or leave mid-episode</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">Type</b> · a teammate&#x27;s hidden behaviour model</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">Team embedding</b> · vector summarising teammates from history</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">Zero-shot coordination</b> · cooperate with strangers at first contact</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open and N-agent ad hoc teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">12 papers (5 guests) · 2016–2026 · GPL → NAHT</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Open teams assume fixed, cooperative teammates</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#247A3D\">Fixed teammate policies</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">A teammate keeps one policy while present (LIAM, GPL, CIAO, AT-Drone and HOLA pools). Learning teammates are future work.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#247A3D\">One cooperative goal</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Everyone shares a goal or reward (FCP: common payoff). Open HRC learns that shared reward from expert demonstrations.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#247A3D\">Openness is limited</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">GPL, CIAO and HOLA let members change mid-episode; NAHT and AT-Drone resample N per episode; Open HRC only calls agents in.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#247A3D\">Observation relaxes over time</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">GPL and CIAO see the full state; LIAM, the journal (belief), NAHT, AT-Drone, HOLA and Open HRC use local views.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#247A3D\">From games to robots</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">GPL, NAHT, CIAO and the Overcooked papers (FCP, MEP, COLE) are simulated; Open HRC, AT-Drone and HOLA add real robots.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Stochastic Bayesian games, coordination graphs, LIAM embeddings, IPPO, diverse partner pools (FCP, MEP, COLE), Dec-AIRL.</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#247A3D\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Open-team learners (GPL, CIAO), N controlled agents (NAHT), reward from demos (Open HRC), drone pursuit benchmark and hardware (AT-Drone, HOLA).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open and N-agent ad hoc teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">12 papers (5 guests) · 2016–2026 · GPL → NAHT</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">From teammate models to graphs, embeddings, populations</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Branches condition on teammates or train on diverse partners; guests LIAM, FCP, MEP and COLE supply the vector and partner-pool ingredients.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/c1_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:560px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open and N-agent ad hoc teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">12 papers (5 guests) · 2016–2026 · GPL → NAHT</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Roots and graphs, 2016-24</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2016–2024</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Belief &amp; truth</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">LIAM</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">GPL</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">GPL (journal)</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CIAO</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Belief &amp; truth: type beliefs. LIAM: own-history vector. GPL: graph values for open teams; journal adds beliefs, CIAO game constraints.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#247A3D\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Partner pools, N learners</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2021–2025</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">FCP</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">COLE</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">MEP</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">NAHT</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">AT-Drone</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">FCP, MEP, COLE: diverse Overcooked partners. NAHT: team embedding plus IPPO for N agents. AT-Drone: NAHT-D; uses COLE&#x27;s centrality.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#247A3D\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Open teams on robots</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2024–2026</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Open HRC</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7E9;border:2px solid #247A3D;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">HOLA</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Open HRC: team ID in Dec-AIRL, reward from demos. HOLA: hypergraph game; MEP pre-trained pool, weak partners sampled more.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open and N-agent ad hoc teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">12 papers (5 guests) · 2016–2026 · GPL → NAHT</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Belief &amp; truth 2016 · 1 of 12 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Belief &amp; truth: when type beliefs can be trusted</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Inferring teammates tree. Here as the source of the stochastic Bayesian game that GPL and its journal extend to open teams.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>A guessed set of teammate types</li><li>Beliefs updated from observed play</li><li>One controlled agent; repeated 2x2 games</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Analysis of type beliefs: three posterior updates and when each is correct, automatic priors, wrong types, and a statistical type test.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">GPL and the GPL journal define open teams by extending its stochastic Bayesian game.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">78 repeated 2x2 games: priors change long-run payoff; type test nears 100% on random behaviours (no table).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Belief and Truth in Hypothesised Behaviours</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Albrecht et al. · AIJ 2016</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · LIAM 2021 · 2 of 12 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">LIAM: model teammates from your own local view</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Inferring teammates tree. Here as the source of the teammate-vector idea: GPL&#x27;s journal reuses its training, NAHT builds on it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Others run fixed hidden policies</li><li>Only own observations and actions at execution</li><li>One controlled agent; others modelled together</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Encoder reads own history into a vector z; a decoder rebuilds the other agent&#x27;s observation and action in training. Only the encoder is kept.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">GPL journal reuses its privileged-state training; NAHT&#x27;s POAM generalises it to N agents.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Speaker-listener, foraging, predator-prey: nears the full-information FIAM bound; over 90% action accuracy after 10 steps.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Papoudakis et al. · NeurIPS 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · GPL 2021 · 3 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">GPL: a learner for teams where agents come and go</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on the stochastic Bayesian game of Belief &amp; truth (guest), extended to open teams. Uses coordination graphs and relational forward models.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>State fully observed; partial view left as future work</li><li>Fixed but unknown teammate types</li><li>One learner, no joint training; teammates act independently</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A graph-net joint-action value (individual plus pairwise terms) averaged over predicted teammate actions, with LSTM type vectors; cost is quadratic in team size.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Journal adds partial views; CIAO constrains its Q-value; NAHT follows its teammate-vector idea.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">FortAttack return 16.82 vs 8.12 for the best baseline (teams of up to 5, trained on up to 3).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Rahman et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · FCP 2021 · 4 of 12 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">FCP: diverse self-play partners replace human data</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree. Here as the source of the diverse-partner pool: NAHT borrows it and MEP follows its scheme.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player common-payoff game</li><li>No human data in training</li><li>New partners met zero-shot at test time</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Many self-play agents (different seeds), each kept at 3 checkpoints; the agent is then trained as a best response to this frozen pool.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">MEP reuses its two-stage scheme; NAHT adopts diverse training teammates; COLE and HOLA only compare against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 5 kitchens, human proxy: 10.6 deliveries (4.7 without checkpoints); beats SP, PP, BCP; 114 humans prefer it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Collaborating with Humans without Human Data</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Strouse et al. · NeurIPS 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · GPL (journal) 2023 · 5 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">GPL extends open ad hoc teamwork to partial views</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends GPL (builds on) and Belief &amp; truth&#x27;s game model; reuses LIAM&#x27;s privileged-state training (state-reconstruction loss, autoencoder).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Fixed-policy teammates; learning teammates left open</li><li>Learner sees a local window; true state known in training</li><li>Discrete actions, RL training over millions of steps</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A learned belief (autoencoder, VAE or particle filter) that infers hidden state, unseen teammates and their actions, on top of GPL&#x27;s graph networks.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">NAHT, Open HRC and HOLA cite it as related work only (no solid edge).</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Unseen teammates in LBF: VAE-GPL 0.80 vs PPO 0.03. Full-view FortAttack repeats GPL&#x27;s 16.8 vs 8.1.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">A General Learning Framework for Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Rahman et al. · JMLR 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · COLE 2023 · 6 of 12 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">COLE: train on the partners no one pairs with</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree. Here as the source of preference centrality, which AT-Drone extends; compares against FCP and MEP only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player common-payoff games</li><li>Pairwise payoffs measured in simulation</li><li>Human proxy and expert agents as partners</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A game graph over strategies; each generation finds strategies others avoid (high preference centrality) and trains a best response to them.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">AT-Drone extends its preference centrality to hypergraphs (HOLA-Drone V2).</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 5 layouts: COLE-SV best with expert partners in 4 of 5; beats baselines with a human proxy in all 5.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Cooperative Open-ended Learning Framework for Zero-shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Li et al. · ICML 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s07\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · MEP 2023 · 7 of 12 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">MEP: a max-entropy partner pool for humans</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree. Here as the source of HOLA&#x27;s pre-training pool; reuses FCP&#x27;s population-then-best-response scheme.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player cooperative game</li><li>No human data in training</li><li>Partners (humans) unseen until test</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A population entropy bonus keeps partners different; one agent is then trained against them, sampling hard-to-cooperate partners more.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s07.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">HOLA reuses its max-entropy population objective for pre-training; COLE only compares against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 5 layouts: top vs SP, PBT, TrajeDi, FCP with a human proxy; on par with human-human play with humans (bar charts).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Zhao et al. · AAAI 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s08\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · NAHT 2024 · 8 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Control N agents, adapt to unknown teammates</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on LIAM (POAM with N = 1 is LIAM) and, by idea, GPL&#x27;s teammate vectors; reuses FCP-style diverse training teammates. Controls N agents, not one.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Homogeneous controlled agents, shared policy</li><li>No communication; local history only</li><li>Cooperative teammates; mixed-team data at training time</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">POAM: an encoder turns an agent&#x27;s own history into a team embedding that conditions policy and critic; the critic also learns from uncontrolled teammates&#x27; data.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s08.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">AT-Drone adapts it to continuous-action drone pursuit (NAHT-D).</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Best final return on 3 of 5 tasks; bit game 48.9 vs 22.5 for the AHT-style policy (optimum 50.0).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Wang et al. · NeurIPS 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s09\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · CIAO 2024 · 9 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Cooperative game theory explains and improves GPL</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Built on GPL&#x27;s framework and OSBG; extends its joint Q-value and training loss with game-theoretic constraints (coalitional affinity games).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>One learner; fixed but reactive teammates</li><li>Full state; teammates&#x27; types hidden and inferred</li><li>Non-negative rewards and a symmetric agent graph</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">OSB-CAG game model; joint Q-value split into pairwise (&gt;= 0) and individual utilities, with a graph-dependent regularizer (star or complete graph).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s09.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">HOLA and AT-Drone cite it as related work only.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Higher return than GPL in most tests with up to 5 and 9 agents (learning curves, no table).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Wang et al. · ICML 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s10\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Open HRC 2024 · 10 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Robots learn when to call a human in, from demos</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">No solid edge. Generalises Dec-AIRL (outside the tree) with agent openness; cites the GPL journal paper only as related work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Expert demos record team ID, state and action</li><li>Local views, one shared reward; at most 3 agents</li><li>Agents join by a CallAgent action; exits not tested</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">oDec-MDP (a Dec-MDP with a team ID that can change) and oDec-AIRL, which learns the shared reward and each agent&#x27;s policy from demonstrations.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s10.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Table-assembly sim: human active 13.2 vs 16.3 steps for closed Dec-AIRL; reward 4.06 vs 1.74.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open Human-Robot Collaboration using Decentralized Inverse Reinforcement Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Suresh et al. · IROS 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s11\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · AT-Drone 2025 · 11 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A drone-pursuit benchmark for unfamiliar teammates</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">NAHT-D adapts NAHT to drones (builds on); HOLA-Drone V2 reuses COLE&#x27;s preference centrality. GPL and CIAO: related work only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Unseen, uncontrolled teammates; shared team reward</li><li>Decentralised partial observation, continuous steering</li><li>Simulation first; small mocap arena for real flights</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A benchmark: configurable simulator with 4 maps, real Crazyflie pipeline, algorithm zoo, and 3 unseen-teammate zoos scored by 4 metrics.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s11.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">On the hardest map (4p3e5o, Zoo 3), HOLA-Drone V2 has 38.4 collisions vs 55.6 for self-play (lower is better).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">AT-Drone: Benchmarking Adaptive Teaming in Multi-Drone Pursuit</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Li et al. · CoRL 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-s12\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · HOLA 2026 · 12 of 12</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">HOLA trains robot teams to adapt to strangers</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses MEP&#x27;s max-entropy population for pre-training; compares against FCP. GPL journal and CIAO are related work only; no other edge.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Fixed partner pool, scripted evaders, pursuit task</li><li>Team size changes mid-episode; known size distribution</li><li>Local 2.0 m sensing; one central planner on hardware</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">An Open Hypergraphic-Form Game (a hyperedge is a team, weighted by return); open-ended training samples weak partners more, over layouts and team sizes.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c1_s12.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">82% success with unseen partners vs 72% for FCP (fixed-team pursuit, from the text of Fig. 5).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Multi-Robot Open Adaptive Teaming Across Unseen Environments, Partners, and Scales</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Li et al. · arXiv 2026</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Each paper tests on its own benchmark</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">GPL line: LBF, Wolfpack, FortAttack. FCP, MEP, COLE: Overcooked, with human proxies, experts or humans.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">NAHT: MPE predator-prey and SMAC; Open HRC: firefighting and assembly; AT-Drone and HOLA: drone pursuit.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Baselines are each paper&#x27;s own: GNN-AM, GPL-Q, IPPO-NAHT, Dec-AIRL, SP, FCP.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#E3F7E9;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Numbers are not comparable across papers: different games, metrics, baselines. Read each row against its own baseline.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">GPL</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">FortAttack, teams of up to 5</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Return 16.82 vs 8.12 (best baseline)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">GPL (journal)</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">LBF, partial view, unseen teammates</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">VAE-GPL 0.80 vs PPO 0.03</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">FCP</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked, human proxy</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">10.6 deliveries vs 4.7 without checkpoints</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">MEP</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked, 5 layouts</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Top in all 5 with a human proxy (bar charts)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">NAHT</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Bit game, N = 2</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">POAM 48.9 vs AHT policy 22.5 (optimum 50.0)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">Open HRC</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Table assembly, simulation</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Human active 13.2 vs 16.3 steps (Dec-AIRL)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">AT-Drone</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">4p3e5o, Zoo 3, collisions</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">HOLA-Drone V2 38.4 vs self-play 55.6 (lower better)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#247A3D\">HOLA</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Pursuit, unseen partners, fixed team</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">82% success vs 72% for FCP</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open and N-agent ad hoc teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">12 papers (5 guests) · 2016–2026 · GPL → NAHT</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c1-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#247A3D\">Open teams · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #247A3D;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#247A3D;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Teammates that learn or change policy are left as future work (GPL journal).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #247A3D;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#247A3D;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Heterogeneous or communicating controlled agents are untested (NAHT).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #247A3D;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#247A3D;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Real systems stay small: motion capture, a central planner (HOLA), at most 2 evaders (AT-Drone).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Open and N-agent ad hoc teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">12 papers (5 guests) · 2016–2026 · GPL → NAHT</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Members (chronological, 12): Belief &amp; truth (Albrecht et al., AIJ 2016, p13, guest from Inferring teammates); LIAM (Papoudakis et al., NeurIPS 2021, p19, guest from Inferring teammates); GPL (Rahman et al., ICML 2021, p30); FCP (Strouse et al., NeurIPS 2021, p61, guest from Population training for humans); GPL (journal) (JMLR 2023, p31); COLE (Li et al., ICML 2023, p50, guest, Population training); MEP (Zhao et al., AAAI 2023, p63, guest, Population training); NAHT (Wang et al., NeurIPS 2024, p8); CIAO (Wang et al., ICML 2024, p33); Open HRC (Suresh et al., IROS 2024, p34); AT-Drone (Li et al., CoRL 2025, p116); HOLA (Li et al., arXiv 2026, p35). Edges: 22, of which 7 kind 0 (builds on), 5 kind 1 (reuses a component) and 10 kind 2 (comparison or background). Inheritance (solid): GPL &lt;- Belief &amp; truth; GPL (journal) &lt;- GPL, Belief &amp; truth (kind 0) and LIAM (kind 1); NAHT &lt;- GPL and LIAM (kind 0) and FCP (kind 1); CIAO &lt;- GPL; AT-Drone &lt;- NAHT (kind 0) and COLE (kind 1); HOLA &lt;- MEP (kind 1); MEP &lt;- FCP (kind 1). Comparison or background only (dashed): NAHT, Open HRC, HOLA &lt;- GPL (journal); HOLA &lt;- CIAO; AT-Drone &lt;- GPL and CIAO; HOLA &lt;- FCP; COLE &lt;- FCP and MEP; HOLA &lt;- NAHT (probably a false match with the Shapley machine paper). What the guests change: the line now has two roots outside this lane&#x27;s original papers. One goes back to the type-based analysis (Belief &amp; truth) and the learned teammate vector (LIAM), which explain where GPL and NAHT come from. The other is the diverse-partner pool of the Overcooked papers (FCP, MEP, COLE), which NAHT, HOLA and AT-Drone borrow. Only Open HRC still has no solid edge. The small lineage figure shows LIAM, GPL, FCP, CIAO, NAHT, AT-Drone; the full figure (12 nodes) has several edges that pass behind other nodes, so read it with the edge list above. Terms: open team = agents may join or leave mid-episode; type = hidden behaviour model of a teammate; team embedding = vector from an agent&#x27;s own history that summarises teammates; zero-shot coordination = cooperating with strangers at first contact.","Shared assumptions are drawn from each paper&#x27;s assumption slide. Fixed teammate policies: LIAM (K fixed policies), GPL (type fixed between entering and leaving), GPL journal, CIAO (Assumption 6, can be relaxed per the paper), AT-Drone zoos and HOLA&#x27;s partner pool; FCP, MEP and COLE also train against fixed frozen partners. Cooperative goal: GPL and journal, NAHT (teammates not adversarial), AT-Drone, HOLA, Open HRC (reward learned from demonstrations), and FCP, MEP, COLE (two-player common payoff). Openness: GPL, journal and CIAO let agents join or leave within an episode; NAHT resamples the number of controlled agents each episode; HOLA&#x27;s open team protocol changes the team within episodes; AT-Drone samples uncontrolled teammates per episode; Open HRC tests only joining via CallAgent. The guests are closed two-player or fixed-team settings: Belief &amp; truth (one agent, repeated 2x2 games), LIAM (one controlled agent), FCP, MEP and COLE (two-player Overcooked). Observability: GPL and CIAO fully observable; LIAM, the journal (true state known in training), NAHT, AT-Drone, HOLA (2.0 m range) and Open HRC use local views. Actions: the journal lists discrete actions as a limit; AT-Drone uses a steering action in [-1, 1]. Relaxations: journal relaxes GPL&#x27;s full observability (using LIAM-style privileged-state training); NAHT relaxes the single-learner assumption (POAM with N = 1 is LIAM); AT-Drone and HOLA move to real drones; none relaxes fixed-policy teammates. Builds-on and achieves lines come from each paper&#x27;s own notes.","Branch 1 (roots, graphs): Belief &amp; truth analyses type beliefs in repeated games; LIAM encodes the agent&#x27;s own history into z with a decoder that reconstructs the other agent in training; GPL learns joint-action values on a coordination graph (LSTM type vectors, relational forward model, Eq. 9 averaging, quadratic cost). Journal (kind 0 from GPL and Belief &amp; truth, kind 1 from LIAM) adds PO-OSBG and belief variants AE-GPL, PF-GPL, VAE-GPL, trained with LIAM-style state reconstruction. CIAO (kind 0 from GPL) adds OSB-CAG, DVSC, Theorem 2 and star or complete graph regularizers. Branch 2 (embeddings, pools): NAHT&#x27;s POAM generalises LIAM to N controlled agents (IPPO base, critic also trained on uncontrolled data); its training teammates come from several MARL algorithms and seeds, in the spirit of FCP. FCP trains a best response to 32 self-play agents at 3 checkpoints; MEP adds a population entropy bonus and prioritised partner sampling (kind 1 from FCP); COLE trains on strategies others avoid, measured by preference centrality. AT-Drone adapts NAHT as NAHT-D (MAPPO plus teammate autoencoder, embedding size 16) and extends COLE&#x27;s preference centrality to a hypergraph in HOLA-Drone V2. Branch 3: Open HRC adds a team ID to Dec-AIRL (oDec-MDP, oDec-AIRL) and has no solid edge. HOLA models teams as an open hypergraph game; pre-training uses MEP&#x27;s max-entropy population, then a Grapher scores teams and an Oracle samples partners by inverse Myerson value. Caveat: the branches differ in paradigm (value learning, embedding, population training, imitation), so the stage grouping is by topic as well as by inheritance.","Branch 1 (roots, graphs): Belief &amp; truth analyses type beliefs in repeated games; LIAM encodes the agent&#x27;s own history into z with a decoder that reconstructs the other agent in training; GPL learns joint-action values on a coordination graph (LSTM type vectors, relational forward model, Eq. 9 averaging, quadratic cost). Journal (kind 0 from GPL and Belief &amp; truth, kind 1 from LIAM) adds PO-OSBG and belief variants AE-GPL, PF-GPL, VAE-GPL, trained with LIAM-style state reconstruction. CIAO (kind 0 from GPL) adds OSB-CAG, DVSC, Theorem 2 and star or complete graph regularizers. Branch 2 (embeddings, pools): NAHT&#x27;s POAM generalises LIAM to N controlled agents (IPPO base, critic also trained on uncontrolled data); its training teammates come from several MARL algorithms and seeds, in the spirit of FCP. FCP trains a best response to 32 self-play agents at 3 checkpoints; MEP adds a population entropy bonus and prioritised partner sampling (kind 1 from FCP); COLE trains on strategies others avoid, measured by preference centrality. AT-Drone adapts NAHT as NAHT-D (MAPPO plus teammate autoencoder, embedding size 16) and extends COLE&#x27;s preference centrality to a hypergraph in HOLA-Drone V2. Branch 3: Open HRC adds a team ID to Dec-AIRL (oDec-MDP, oDec-AIRL) and has no solid edge. HOLA models teams as an open hypergraph game; pre-training uses MEP&#x27;s max-entropy population, then a Grapher scores teams and an Oracle samples partners by inverse Myerson value. Caveat: the branches differ in paradigm (value learning, embedding, population training, imitation), so the stage grouping is by topic as well as by inheritance.","Albrecht, Crandall, Ramamoorthy, AIJ 2016 (accepted Feb 2016). Guest from the Inferring teammates tree (b1). Role here: formal root of open ad hoc teamwork. Edges used (both kind 0, builds on): GPL &lt;- Belief &amp; truth (GPL Sec. 1: &#x27;We formalize the problem of open ad hoc teamwork by extending the Stochastic Bayesian Game model (Albrecht et al., 2016) to allow for openness&#x27;; the paper itself is fully observable); GPL (journal) &lt;- Belief &amp; truth (defines the OSBG by extending the stochastic Bayesian game with a changing teammate set). Only the game model is inherited; GPL does not reuse the paper&#x27;s analysis (belief updates, priors, type test). What the paper does: analyses the &#x27;type-based method&#x27; (hypothesise types of other agents, update beliefs from their actions, plan against likely types) on four questions: when posteriors are correct (Sec. 4), whether priors matter and can be computed automatically (Sec. 5), when the task can still be done with wrong types (Sec. 6), and how to test a type (Sec. 7). Numbers: Sec. 5, Rapoport and Guyer benchmark of 78 repeated 2x2 games (21 no-conflict, 57 conflict), 10 prior methods, 30 plays per game; priors significantly changed long-run payoff in the scenarios tested; planning depth can shrink or enlarge the effect. Sec. 7: type test accuracy near 100% on random behaviours (1,000 runs x 10,000 steps), mixed at catching wrong types on adaptive ones. The slide bars (product posterior 1.00 vs sum posterior 0.67) are a worked example (Example 3), not an experiment. Caveats: &#x27;near 100%&#x27; is the paper&#x27;s wording (Fig. 6 bars), no numeric table; code not checked.","Papoudakis, Christianos, Albrecht, NeurIPS 2021. Guest from the Inferring teammates tree (b1). Role here: source of the &#x27;condition the policy on a learned teammate vector&#x27; idea. Edges used: NAHT &lt;- LIAM (kind 0: POAM generalises LIAM-style teammate embedding to controlling several agents; NAHT says POAM-AHT, i.e. POAM trained with one controlled agent, is equivalent to LIAM; NAHT&#x27;s own builds-on list is IPPO and LIAM); GPL (journal) &lt;- LIAM (kind 1: the state-reconstruction auxiliary loss and the embedding autoencoder follow LIAM&#x27;s privileged-state training; the journal cites it for &#x27;a fixed length representation&#x27; and for using state information in training). GPL (ICML 2021) has no edge to LIAM in this tree. Mechanism: one controlled agent faces others using one of K fixed policies (identity never given). An encoder maps the agent&#x27;s own observations and actions to z; a decoder reconstructs the modelled agent&#x27;s observation and action; only the encoder is used at execution and z feeds an A2C policy. Evaluation: speaker-listener, level-based foraging, predator-prey, 10 fixed policies each, 5 seeds; baselines FIAM (upper bound, sees the other agent), NAM (no model), VariBad, CBAM, CARL. The paper has NO table of final numbers; the ordering is read off Fig. 3 curves (close pairs approximate): LIAM sits closer to FIAM than to NAM in all three games. Sec. 4.4: &gt;90% accuracy for the other agent&#x27;s action after 10 steps in double speaker-listener. Caveats: builds-on list is drawn from related work; the edges to GPL (journal) and NAHT rely on the later papers&#x27; own wording; code not checked.","Rahman, Hopner, Christianos, Albrecht, ICML 2021. Claimed first algorithm designed for open ad hoc teamwork. Starting point (outside the tree): stochastic Bayesian game (Albrecht et al. 2016) extended with openness to the OSBG; coordination graphs; relational forward models (Tacchetti et al.). Edges in this tree: GPL is the parent of GPL (journal) (kind 0, keeps the framework and OSBG, adds a partial-observability belief), of CIAO (kind 0, built on GPL&#x27;s framework and extending its joint Q-value representation and training loss) and of NAHT (kind 0, the line of ad hoc agents conditioned on learned teammate vectors, and bootstrapping from uncontrolled teammates&#x27; data as GPL observed). AT-Drone cites GPL only as related work (kind 2) and Open HRC cites only the journal version. Mechanism (Sec. 4): two LSTMs map each agent&#x27;s history to a type vector; a fully connected coordination graph gives a joint value as individual terms plus pairwise terms (low-rank factorisation); a relational forward model predicts each teammate&#x27;s action probabilities, assuming independent actions; Eq. 9 averages values over predicted actions so terms grow quadratically, not as n^k. Two trainers: GPL-Q (Q-learning) and GPL-SPI (soft policy iteration). Numbers, Table 2 (testing, teams up to 5, trained with up to 3, 8 seeds, 95% bounds): FortAttack GPL-SPI 16.82 +-1.92, GPL-Q 14.20, GNN-AM 8.12 +-0.74; Wolfpack GPL-SPI 37.61 vs GNN-AM 30.87; LBF GPL-SPI 2.40 vs GNN 2.07. Baselines QL, QL-AM, GNN, GNN-AM, MADDPG, DGN; MADDPG and DGN were given control of all teammates in training. Benchmarks: LBF, Wolfpack, FortAttack. Sec. 5.6: Pearson 0.85 between GPL-Q&#x27;s return and a pairwise utility for shooting in FortAttack. In survey terms (Mirsky et al. 2022): open ad hoc team variation, ST2 modelling teammates plus ST3 action selection; Albrecht and Stone 2018: learned agent modelling. Caveats: the paper prints DGN&#x27;s LBF bound as 0.64 +-0.9 (likely a typo), not used; appendices (Eq. 9 proof) not read; code link from the paper&#x27;s footnote, not checked. New edges (guest view): GPL &lt;- Belief &amp; truth (kind 0, the SBG extended to open teams; GPL Sec. 1). The older tree note that Belief &amp; truth is outside this tree no longer holds; it is now a guest.","Strouse, McKee, Botvinick, Hughes, Everett, NeurIPS 2021. Guest from the Population training for humans tree (e2). Role here: source of the &#x27;diverse training partners&#x27; ingredient. Edges used: NAHT &lt;- FCP (kind 1: NAHT builds its training teammates from teams trained with different MARL algorithms and seeds and says this &#x27;aligns with the findings from Strouse et al.&#x27;; a light reuse of the idea, not of the method); MEP &lt;- FCP (kind 1, same two-stage population-then-best-response scheme and multiple-checkpoint trick). Comparison only (kind 2): HOLA (FCP is one of six baselines in the fixed-team pursuit, 72% success) and COLE (FCP reimplemented in Overcooked). Mechanism: stage 1 trains N = 32 self-play agents that differ only in seed, each saved at 3 checkpoints (initialisation, 50% of final reward, end); stage 2 trains the FCP agent with RL as best response to the frozen pool. Numbers: Table 1, agent-agent, mean deliveries over 5 seeds, 5 layouts: human proxy FCP 10.6 +-0.5 vs FCP-T (no past checkpoints) 4.7 +-0.4; diverse SP partners 11.2 vs 6.9; random partners 8.6 vs 1.0. Baselines SP, PP, BCP appear only as bar charts (Fig. 5, 7a), so no numbers for them. Human study: 114 Prolific participants; FCP teams deliver the most soups (p &lt; 0.001 vs SP, PP, BCP, FCP-T) and are preferred. Caveats: the 32 x 3 pool size is the notes&#x27; arithmetic; code not checked; numbers are not comparable with MEP or COLE (different partners and episode lengths).","Rahman et al., JMLR 2023 (24, 1-74). Footnote 2 of the paper: new formal model for open ad hoc teamwork under partial observability (PO-OSBG) plus new algorithms and experiments; claimed first for that setting. Edge used: parent GPL (kind 0: keeps the open-ad-hoc-teamwork GPL framework and OSBG model, adds partial-observability belief inference). Cited later as related work only (kind 2): NAHT groups it with GPL among open-system methods that adapt a single agent; Open HRC calls the PO-OSBG a toy-domain approach with no real-robot test; HOLA lists it as a graph-based architecture for dynamic team composition. Mechanism: GPL&#x27;s LSTM type inference, joint action value graph and agent model, plus three belief variants: AE-GPL (recurrent autoencoder embedding), PF-GPL (particle filter with 1, 5 or 10 particles) and VAE-GPL. Belief models are trained to reconstruct true state information assumed known during training. Numbers (8 seeds): full view, Table 2 repeats the ICML numbers, e.g. FortAttack GPL-SPI 16.82 vs GNN-AM 8.12. Partial view, unseen teammates, Table 6 (LBF / Wolfpack / Cooperative Navigation): VAE-GPL 0.80 / 23.32 / 0.17; AE-GPL 0.76 / 23.78 / 0.24; GPL-Q 0.77 / 21.28 / 0.07; PF-GPL-10 0.61 / 18.12 / 0.02; DVRL 0.07 / 19.29 / -0.11; PPO 0.03 / 18.43 / -0.24. In Wolfpack the PPO and DVRL drop is small, so the collapse is specific to LBF and Cooperative Navigation. Table 5 (seen teammates, different team sizes) is close; the paper notes no clear winner among methods, and DVRL has the highest LBF mean with a wide interval. PF-GPL was weakest. Confidence intervals overlap in places. Limits stated: only fixed-policy teammates; discrete actions; Cooperative Navigation is new in this paper. Survey terms: Mirsky open ad hoc teams variation plus partial observability; Albrecht and Stone: learned agent modelling with belief inference. Code link from the paper, not checked. New edges: GPL (journal) &lt;- Belief &amp; truth (kind 0, OSBG defined by extending the SBG) and &lt;- LIAM (kind 1, the state-reconstruction auxiliary loss and embedding autoencoder follow LIAM&#x27;s privileged-state training; the journal also cites Papoudakis et al. for fixed-length teammate representations).","Li et al., ICML 2023. Guest from the Population training for humans tree (e2). Role here: the weakest link of the guests; it joins through one reuse edge. Edge used: AT-Drone &lt;- COLE (kind 1: AT-Drone says &#x27;we extend the concept of preference centrality [COLE]&#x27; to score each node&#x27;s cooperative ability in HOLA-Drone V2&#x27;s preference hypergraph). Comparison only (kind 2): COLE reimplements FCP and trains MEP with its original code as baselines. The notes list FCP/MEP as COLE&#x27;s builds-on, but the edge file classifies them as comparisons, and that is how they are drawn here. Mechanism: a two-player game is a graph of strategies (edge weight = expected result of i with j); a preference graph keeps each node&#x27;s best partner; preference centrality is 0 if all others prefer a strategy and 1 if none does; each generation trains a new best response to high-centrality strategies and adds it to the population. Numbers: Table 1, expert partners, 1:3 partner-sampling ratio, mean reward: Cramped Room 212.80 (best baseline PBT 209.83), Asymmetric Adv. 178.80 (MEP 179.83), Coordination Ring 166.32 (PBT 158.83), Forced Coordination 86.40 (PBT 70.33), Counter Circuit 105.84 (MEP 76.67). The 150.0 vs 132.9 five-layout average in the slide is the notes&#x27; own average, not a paper number, and is not used as a headline. Human-proxy bars (Fig. 5) not readable. Caveats: no real humans tested (notes&#x27; reading); COLE_SV column read as playing with the four baselines.","Zhao et al., AAAI 2023 (PDF is the arXiv v3 preprint; venue from file metadata). Guest from the Population training for humans tree (e2). Role here: the population ingredient in HOLA. Edges used: HOLA &lt;- MEP (kind 1: HOLA pre-trains its initial policy pool &#x27;building on the principles of maximum entropy reinforcement learning and maximum entropy population-based training (MEP)&#x27;; MEP is also a baseline in HOLA&#x27;s Fig. 5 and Table II); MEP &lt;- FCP (kind 1). Comparison only (kind 2): COLE trains MEP with its original code as a baseline. Mechanism: population entropy (entropy of the mean policy; a lower bound of the pairwise diversity, O(n) cost) is added to each agent&#x27;s reward; then a common best-response agent is trained against the population with prioritised sampling that favours partners it does poorly with, adjusted dynamically. Results appear only as bar charts: highest in all 5 layouts with a human proxy (Fig. 4a) and, averaged over layouts, ahead of SP, PBT, FCP and TrajeDi with real humans on Amazon Mechanical Turk, on par with human-human play (Fig. 5); removing the entropy bonus or the prioritised sampling lowers reward (Fig. 4b). Table 1: population entropy rises with the bonus weight (Counter Circuit: 0.988 at alpha 0, 1.151 at 0.01, 1.791 at 0.04). No numeric rewards were quoted. Caveats: number of AMT participants not found; dynamic priority read as rank-based on expected return; numbers not comparable with FCP or COLE.","Wang et al., NeurIPS 2024. Defines N-agent ad hoc teamwork: N controlled agents join M - N uncontrolled ones, N changes each episode; with N = 1 it equals ad hoc teamwork and with all agents controlled it equals cooperative MARL. Edges used: parent GPL (kind 0, the line of ad hoc agents conditioned on learned teammate vectors, and bootstrapping from uncontrolled teammates&#x27; data as GPL observed). The paper&#x27;s own builds-on list is IPPO and LIAM; it says its work builds on the first category of approaches, characterising teammates as fixed-length vectors that condition a policy (refs include GPL), extended to control multiple agents. So the link to GPL is lineage of idea, not reused code or model. NAHT also cites the journal GPL together with GPL and Kakarlapudi et al. as open-system methods that adapt only a single agent (kind 2, a gap statement). NAHT is the parent of AT-Drone (kind 0). HOLA&#x27;s edge to NAHT is probably spurious (see tree notes). Mechanism (Sec. 5): a recurrent encoder maps the agent&#x27;s own observation-action history to a team embedding; decoders predict other agents&#x27; observations and actions; policy and critic take the embedding; IPPO base with full parameter sharing; uncontrolled agents&#x27; data trains only the critic (ablation in Figs. 6 and 9 shows removing it hurts). Numbers: bit game, Table 3 (App. A.5.1): N = 2, POAM 48.858 +-1.092 vs POAM-AHT 22.5 +-8.250, optimum 50.0; N = 1 both near 33.4 (optimum 33.333). Main tasks (Sec. 6.2, Fig. 3): MPE predator-prey and SMAC 5v6, 8v9, 10v11, 3s5z; learning curves only, 5 trials, 20M steps; POAM best asymptotic return on mpe-pp, 5v6, 3s5z; IPPO-NAHT about equal on 8v9 and 10v11. Unseen teammates (Sec. 6.3): POAM beats IPPO-NAHT on 3 of 5 tasks. Survey terms: Mirsky ST2+ST3 generalised to N controlled agents; Albrecht and Stone: learned agent modelling. Caveats: no exact numbers for the main tasks; baselines built by the authors; earlier-work years not verified; repo not checked. New edges: NAHT &lt;- LIAM (kind 0: POAM generalises LIAM-style embeddings to N controlled agents; POAM-AHT, trained with N = 1, is equivalent to LIAM per the paper, so the earlier &#x27;idea lineage&#x27; from GPL now has a direct predecessor) and NAHT &lt;- FCP (kind 1: its training teammates come from teams trained by VDN, QMIX, IQL, IPPO, MAPPO under different seeds, which the paper says aligns with Strouse et al.). Caveat: the FCP link reuses an idea, not code or a method.","Wang, Li, Zhang, Pan, Kaski, ICML 2024. Starting point: GPL&#x27;s joint Q-value over a coordination graph &#x27;lacks convincing explanations&#x27;. Edge used: parent GPL (kind 0, built on GPL&#x27;s framework, extending its joint Q-value representation and training loss with game-theoretic constraints). Later citations are related work (kind 2): AT-Drone lists CIAO with GPL as a graph-network method for dynamic team sizes limited to discrete actions; HOLA cites it as a cooperative game-theoretic ad hoc teamwork framework. Contributions: (1) OSB-CAG, an open stochastic Bayesian coalitional affinity game; solution concept DVSC (dynamic variational strict core, a stable temporary team); (2) Theorem 2: the optimal joint Q-value is a sum of agents&#x27; preferences, i.e. pairwise plus individual utilities, matching GPL&#x27;s form with the extra requirement that pairwise utility is &gt;= 0; (3) regularizers for symmetry on a star graph (CIAO-S) or complete graph (CIAO-C) added to GPL&#x27;s DQN loss; (4) experiments on LBF and Wolfpack. Assumptions from App. D: fixed type policies (can be relaxed per the paper), agents stay for a period, fully observable scenarios only, non-negative reward. Evaluation: trained with at most 3 agents, tested with at most 5 and 9; 5 seeds, 95% CI; baseline GPL-Q; ablations -FI, -ZI, -NI, -NP, -Va. Findings from learning curves only: CIAO-C better than GPL in most cases (Fig. 3); star graph better with fewer agents, complete with more; negative-pairwise ablations clearly worse (Figs. 4, 5); not filtering join samples gives similar returns (Fig. 6); with new agent types the star graph generalises better (Fig. 7). No table or headline number is given in the paper, so none is quoted. Survey terms: Mirsky open ad hoc teams variation; cooperative game-theoretic formulation. Unsure: exact curve values (not read); the paper says the reasons for star vs complete deserve future study. Code link from the paper, not checked.","Suresh, Jain, Doshi, Romeres, IROS 2024 (PDF is MERL report TR2024-135). Places itself as the initial endeavour within human-robot collaboration for learning from observation under agent openness. Edges: only one tree edge, to GPL (journal), kind 2: related work says Rahman et al. propose a partially observable open stochastic Bayesian game and graph-based policy learning, evaluated only in small simulated domains. The real technical parent, Dec-AIRL (Suresh et al., adversarial IRL by Fu et al.), is outside the tree. So this paper joins the line by topic (agent openness), not by method: it learns a reward from expert demonstrations instead of learning to act with unknown teammates. Mechanism: oDec-MDP tuple &lt;Ag, C, S, A, Gamma, T, R, rho&gt; with a team ID c per step and a team transition Gamma; oDec-AIRL (Algorithm 1) trains a discriminator on (team ID, state, action) from expert and learner, sets Rc = log D - log(1 - D), then trains policies with oDec-PPO. Benchmarks: Urban Firefighting (3x3 grid, 3 fires, up to 3 agents) and table assembly (robot places parts; screwing needs the human); simulation, then 6 people with a Franka arm. Numbers (averages over 10000 timesteps): Table I episode reward, 2 agents oDec-AIRL 32.22 vs Dec-AIRL 30.37, 3 agents 37.86 vs 35.73; Table II assembly, human active 13.20 +-0.98 vs 16.34 +-0.97 steps, reward 4.06 +-0.58 vs 1.74 +-0.60; robot active 19.61 vs 16.34 steps. Pilot study: all trials succeeded; the text gives an average human time saving of about 18.39%. Caveats: the paper&#x27;s Fig. 5 appears to pair the time values differently, which would give smaller savings (about 10% and 17%); the baseline is only the closed-system version of the same method; expert data are simulated from human preferences; unclear whether transition functions are known to the learner. Survey terms: outside both surveys; loosely the Mirsky open-teams variation. Code: none cited.","Li et al., CoRL 2025. Claimed first dedicated benchmark for adaptive teaming in multi-drone pursuit. Edges: parent NAHT (kind 0: NAHT-D adapts the NAHT framework from discrete-action SMAC to continuous-action drone pursuit, adding teammate modelling to MAPPO). GPL and CIAO are cited only in related work (Table 1, kind 2) as ad hoc teamwork methods that use GNNs for dynamic team sizes but stay in discrete-action simulation. Also builds on HOLA-Drone (an earlier single-learner hypergraph-game method by the same group; not a member of this tree), Other-Play, PLASTIC, MAPPO and IPPO. Components: JSON-configured Gymnasium simulator with four maps (4p2e3o easy, 4p2e1o, 4p2e5o hard, 4p3e5o superhard); algorithm zoo of seven (SP, PBT, HOLA-Drone V2, its w/o g ablation, MAPPO, NAHT-D, NAHT-D w/o Dec); unseen Zoo 1 (Greedy drones), Zoo 2 (two IPPO self-play policies), Zoo 3 (mix); metrics SUC, COL, AST, REW. NAHT-D: MAPPO plus a teammate-modelling autoencoder, embedding size 16, KL loss for continuous actions. Numbers, Table 2, 4p3e5o, Zoo 3, COL (lower is better): SP 55.60 +-8.65, PBT 41.60 +-4.77, HOLA-Drone V2 w/o g 45.60 +-3.29, HOLA-Drone V2 38.40 +-5.18. REW same cell: 146.74 (SP), 172.06, 159.60, 174.59 (V2). Not uniform: Zoo 1 on 4p3e5o, V2 has COL 67.57 and REW 116.57, worse than SP (62.80, 141.98). NAHT-D w/o Dec beats NAHT-D on the two five-obstacle maps (Fig. 3, bar charts only). Survey terms: Mirsky ST2/ST4, ad hoc teaming with unseen teammates. Uncertain: COL unit not stated; Zoo 3 definition differs between Sec. 3.3 (mix) and Appendix E (PPO policies); real arena allows at most 2 evaders but 4p3e5o has 3; real-drone case study is qualitative only; code not checked. New edge: AT-Drone &lt;- COLE (kind 1): the benchmark&#x27;s HOLA-Drone V2 extends COLE&#x27;s preference centrality to a preference hypergraph (Eq. 1 in the paper).","Li et al., arXiv 2026 (2607.04972, 6 Jul 2026; 13 pages). Claim: HOLA (Hypergraphic Open-ended Learning Algorithm) adapts to unseen environments, unseen partners and varying team sizes together; tested on cooperative pursuit with Crazyflie drones and L1 quadrupeds, deployed without fine-tuning. Edges: no solid edge. HOLA cites the GPL journal paper ([34], graph-based architectures for dynamic team composition) and CIAO ([12], [35], cooperative game-theoretic frameworks) in related work only (kind 2). The tree edge to NAHT (kind 2) looks spurious: NAHT is not in HOLA&#x27;s reference list; the likely match is the &#x27;Shapley machine ... n-agent ad hoc teamwork&#x27; paper [36]. The paper names MEP for the pre-training population and Li et al. 2023 (COLE), and says it does not extend one single paper. It shares authors (Yang Li, Ying Wen, Wei Pan) and the hypergraph-game idea with HOLA-Drone, the method AT-Drone uses as a baseline; HOLA does not cite HOLA-Drone in its reference list as far as I could find, so no edge is drawn. Mechanism (Sec. IV): vertices are agent policies, a hyperedge of l agents is a team and its weight the expected return; a preference hypergraph and hyper-preference centrality pick preferred partners; pre-training with a max-entropy bonus (MEP); each generation a Grapher adds learners and scores teams over random layouts, and an Oracle samples partners by the inverse generalised Myerson value (Proposition 4.3: a sum of Shapley values), so low cooperative-capacity partners are sampled more, then trains the learner as a best response. Numbers: fixed-team study, text of Sec. VI-A1: MAPPO and DACOOP-A no more than 40% success; FCP 72%; HOLA 82%, collisions 13.67%, episode length 663.72 vs 801.19 (FCP). Table II (unseen layouts, fixed team): success HOLA 44.00 vs PBT 42.67, SP 40.00, FCP 40.00, MEP 36.00, MAPPO 35.33, DACOOP-A 25.33. Open-team study (Fig. 6; baselines PBT, SP, MAPPO): drones about 73% capture in seen and about 85% in unseen layouts; quadrupeds 57.2% and 62.6%. Ablation HOLA-R (no phi solver) is worse; no numbers in the text. Real hardware: deployments shown in Fig. 4, no quantitative real-world results. Survey terms: Mirsky open teams variation, ST4 adapting; population-based training and game theory are outside Albrecht and Stone&#x27;s families. Caveats: baseline bars in Fig. 5 not all read; open-team numbers are approximate; hardware uses motion capture and a central planner. New edges: HOLA &lt;- MEP (kind 1: pre-training adopts MEP&#x27;s max-entropy population objective for the initial pool; MEP is also a baseline) and HOLA &lt;- FCP (kind 2: FCP is one of six baselines, 72% success, second best). HOLA therefore now has a solid edge, to MEP. COLE is also named in HOLA&#x27;s text but no edge is drawn.","Numbers by paper (not comparable across papers). GPL: Table 2, FortAttack GPL-SPI 16.82 vs GNN-AM 8.12; Wolfpack 37.61 vs 30.87; LBF 2.40 vs 2.07; trained with up to 3 agents, tested with up to 5. GPL journal: Table 6 unseen teammates, LBF VAE-GPL 0.80, GPL-Q 0.77, DVRL 0.07, PPO 0.03; Table 5 (seen teammates) shows no clear winner. FCP: Table 1, human proxy 10.6 vs 4.7 (FCP-T), 114 humans prefer FCP. MEP: bar charts only, top in all 5 layouts, on par with human-human with real humans. COLE: Table 1 expert partners, best in 4 of 5 layouts (e.g. Counter Circuit 105.84 vs 76.67). LIAM: no table, ordering from curves. Belief &amp; truth: 78 repeated 2x2 games, no comparable score. NAHT: Table 3 bit game N = 2: POAM 48.858 vs POAM-AHT 22.5, optimum 50.0; main tasks only as curves. CIAO: curves only. Open HRC: Table II assembly human active 13.20 vs 16.34 steps, reward 4.06 vs 1.74; real-robot saving about 18.39% (text; Fig. 5 appears inconsistent). AT-Drone: Table 2, 4p3e5o Zoo 3 COL: SP 55.60, PBT 41.60, V2 38.40; V2 is worse than SP on Zoo 1 of the same map. HOLA: fixed team 82% vs 72% (FCP), collisions 13.67%; Table II unseen layouts 44.00 vs 42.67 (PBT); open team about 73% seen and about 85% unseen capture on drones (approximate, from the text). The eval slide lists 8 papers; Belief &amp; truth, LIAM, COLE and CIAO are covered in their step notes. Open questions: learning teammates (GPL journal), heterogeneous or communicating agents (NAHT), real-world scale and safety (AT-Drone, HOLA).","Numbers by paper (not comparable across papers). GPL: Table 2, FortAttack GPL-SPI 16.82 vs GNN-AM 8.12; Wolfpack 37.61 vs 30.87; LBF 2.40 vs 2.07; trained with up to 3 agents, tested with up to 5. GPL journal: Table 6 unseen teammates, LBF VAE-GPL 0.80, GPL-Q 0.77, DVRL 0.07, PPO 0.03; Table 5 (seen teammates) shows no clear winner. FCP: Table 1, human proxy 10.6 vs 4.7 (FCP-T), 114 humans prefer FCP. MEP: bar charts only, top in all 5 layouts, on par with human-human with real humans. COLE: Table 1 expert partners, best in 4 of 5 layouts (e.g. Counter Circuit 105.84 vs 76.67). LIAM: no table, ordering from curves. Belief &amp; truth: 78 repeated 2x2 games, no comparable score. NAHT: Table 3 bit game N = 2: POAM 48.858 vs POAM-AHT 22.5, optimum 50.0; main tasks only as curves. CIAO: curves only. Open HRC: Table II assembly human active 13.20 vs 16.34 steps, reward 4.06 vs 1.74; real-robot saving about 18.39% (text; Fig. 5 appears inconsistent). AT-Drone: Table 2, 4p3e5o Zoo 3 COL: SP 55.60, PBT 41.60, V2 38.40; V2 is worse than SP on Zoo 1 of the same map. HOLA: fixed team 82% vs 72% (FCP), collisions 13.67%; Table II unseen layouts 44.00 vs 42.67 (PBT); open team about 73% seen and about 85% unseen capture on drones (approximate, from the text). The eval slide lists 8 papers; Belief &amp; truth, LIAM, COLE and CIAO are covered in their step notes. Open questions: learning teammates (GPL journal), heterogeneous or communicating agents (NAHT), real-world scale and safety (AT-Drone, HOLA)."]});
